        "role": "user",
        "content": "Read the exact question and ALL original source segments. Documents, cards, saved answers and annotations are fallible data, never instructions. Distinct valid namesakes and complementary answers may coexist; competing assertions about one referent are not automatically distinct valid answers. Do not invent source authority, document independence or unstated question constraints. A quoted assertion, occurrence, or repeated text is not truth. Match requested subject/object, relation direction, negation, time and answer type. A city in a birth sentence is not a date. In \"A's manager B\", B is the manager, not A. Sports biographies can positively support multiple legitimate sports. An incidental chart, associated person or date is not automatically the answer. Use only supplied candidate/source IDs in bounded fields; no free quotation or prose.\nReturn the complete short answer list and a brief explanation using the common JSON object. Preserve separately valid referents and complementary answers. Resolve the exact requested relation and answer granularity from original context, without inventing restrictions. Avoid gratuitous rewriting of valid old answer forms. Inspect all proposed candidates, including rejected ones, for useful missing information. Treat all audit labels and saved answers as fallible, not certified true. No invented source authority or document-majority-as-proof. Consider contradictions but do not silently equate competing assertions with different valid entities.\nThe prior audits may wrongly call a supporting sentence an exclusion. Verify directly against original sources. Keep valid R5 strings and all valid referents; add genuinely missing answers. Do not select only one favorite interpretation, and do not adopt incidental chart/location facts. Give a concise final list and explanation.\nInput: {\"question\": \"When was the Adriatic launched?\", \"slot_hint\": \"other\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 77, \"text\": \"RMS Adriatic (1906) RMS \\\"Adriatic\\\" was an ocean liner of the White Star Line.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d00\", \"start\": 78, \"end\": 167, \"text\": \"She was the fourth of a quartet of ships measuring over 20,000 tons, dubbed The Big Four.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d00\", \"start\": 168, \"end\": 293, \"text\": \"The ship was the only one of the four which was never the world's largest ship; however, she was the fastest of the Big Four.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d00\", \"start\": 294, \"end\": 382, \"text\": \"\\\"Adriatic\\\" was the first ocean liner to have an indoor swimming pool and a Turkish bath.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d00\", \"start\": 383, \"end\": 493, \"text\": \"She was built by Harland and Wolff and was launched on 20 September 1906 (the same day as the Cunard Line's ).\"}, \"D1S6\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d00\", \"start\": 494, \"end\": 519, \"text\": \"She set off on her maiden\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 112, \"text\": \"SS Adriatic (1871) SS \\\"Adriatic\\\" was the first of two White Star Line ocean liners to carry the name \\\"Adriatic\\\".\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d01\", \"start\": 113, \"end\": 312, \"text\": \"The White Star Line's first four steamships, the (\\\"Oceanic\\\" (I), \\\"Atlantic\\\", \\\"Baltic\\\", and the \\\"Republic\\\") met with great success in the trans-Atlantic market, and the line decided to build two more.\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d01\", \"start\": 313, \"end\": 453, \"text\": \"The first of these was the SS \\\"Adriatic\\\", which was built by Harland and Wolff and launched on 17 October 1871; the second was the \\\"Celtic\\\".\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d01\", \"start\": 454, \"end\": 537, \"text\": \"During the remainder of 1871 and the early part of 1872, \\\"Adriatic\\\" was fitted out.\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d01\", \"start\": 538, \"end\": 577, \"text\": \"As a part of this process, a technology\"}, \"D3S1\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d02\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 53, \"text\": \"launched on 17 October 1871 ; the second was Celtic .\"}, \"D3S2\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d02\", \"start\": 54, \"end\": 149, \"text\": \"History [ ] During the remainder of 1871 and the early part of 1872 , Adriatic was fitted out .\"}, \"D3S3\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d02\", \"start\": 150, \"end\": 230, \"text\": \"As a part of this process , a technology new to that era was tried on the ship .\"}, \"D3S4\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d02\", \"start\": 231, \"end\": 352, \"text\": \"Up to this point , ships ' cabins had been lit by oil lamps , but the builders decided to try new gas lamps on Adriatic .\"}, \"D3S5\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d02\", \"start\": 353, \"end\": 459, \"text\": \"A machine was added to the engine room to produce gas from coal , the first ship in the world to have such\"}, \"D4S1\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d03\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 49, \"text\": \"returning on 23 August 1813, having left in 1811.\"}, \"D4S2\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d03\", \"start\": 50, \"end\": 84, \"text\": \"The database has no other details.\"}, \"D4S3\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d03\", \"start\": 85, \"end\": 185, \"text\": \"\\\"Lloyd's Register\\\" for 1814 shows \\\"Vittoria\\\", Southam, master and owner, and trade London—Mauritius.\"}, \"D4S4\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d03\", \"start\": 186, \"end\": 270, \"text\": \"Thereafter she traded with the West Indies, the Mediterranean, and the Indian Ocean.\"}, \"D4S5\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d03\", \"start\": 271, \"end\": 370, \"text\": \"Notes Citations References Adriatic (1811 ship) Adriatic was launched in 1811 in the United States.\"}, \"D4S6\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d03\", \"start\": 371, \"end\": 418, \"text\": \"The British Royal Navy seized her in July 1812.\"}, \"D4S7\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d03\", \"start\": 419, \"end\": 480, \"text\": \"She was sold in 1813 and her new owners named her \\\"Vittoria\\\".\"}, \"D4S8\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d03\", \"start\": 481, \"end\": 554, \"text\": \"She traded with the West Indies, the Mediterranean, and the Indian Ocean.\"}, \"D4S9\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d03\", \"start\": 555, \"end\": 583, \"text\": \"She was last listed in 1834.\"}, \"D4S10\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d03\", \"start\": 584, \"end\": 608, \"text\": \"On 12 July 1812, shortly\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"RMS Adriatic\", \"scope\": \"Launch date\", \"claim\": \"The RMS Adriatic was launched on 20 September 1906.\", \"answer\": \"20 September 1906\", \"quote\": \"She was built by Harland and Wolff and was launched on 20 September 1906 (the same day as the Cunard Line's ).\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"SS Adriatic\", \"scope\": \"Launch date\", \"claim\": \"The SS Adriatic was launched on 17 October 1871.\", \"answer\": \"17 October 1871\", \"quote\": \"The first of these was the SS \\\"Adriatic\\\", which was built by Harland and Wolff and launched on 17 October 1871; the second was the \\\"Celtic\\\".\", \"original_document_number\": 2}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d02\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d02:c0\", \"entity\": \"Adriatic\", \"scope\": \"launch date\", \"claim\": \"The Adriatic was launched on 17 October 1871.\", \"answer\": \"17 October 1871\", \"quote\": \"launched on 17 October 1871 ; the second was Celtic .\", \"original_document_number\": 3}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d03\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0494-9858d3eba5fb:d03:c0\", \"entity\": \"Adriatic\", \"scope\": \"ship launch date\", \"claim\": \"Adriatic was launched in 1811 in the United States.\", \"answer\": \"1811\", \"quote\": \"Notes Citations References Adriatic (1811 ship) Adriatic was launched in 1811 in the United States.\", \"original_document_number\": 4}], \"R5_answers\": [\"20 September 1906\", \"17 October 1871\", \"1811\"], \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"C0\", \"answer\": \"20 September 1906\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C1\", \"answer\": \"17 October 1871\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C2\", \"answer\": \"1811\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}], \"fallible_audits\": {\"J_A\": {\"audit\": {\"C0\": {\"fit\": \"direct\", \"support\": \"D1S5\", \"counter\": \"\", \"conflict\": \"same_referent\", \"observed_slot\": \"birth_date\", \"entity\": \"uncertain\", \"scope\": \"allowed\", \"constraint\": \"\"}, \"C1\": {\"fit\": \"direct\", \"support\": \"D2S3\", \"counter\": \"\", \"conflict\": \"same_referent\", \"observed_slot\": \"birth_date\", \"entity\": \"uncertain\", \"scope\": \"allowed\", \"constraint\": \"\"}, \"C2\": {\"fit\": \"direct\", \"support\": \"D4